// Title: Potential Linux Process Code Injection Via DD Utility
// Sigma ID: 4cad6c64-d6df-42d6-8dae-eb78defdc415
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Joseph Kamau
// Date: 2023-12-01 | Modified: 2026-11-21
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, stealth, T1055.009
// Description: Detects the injection of code by overwriting the memory map of a Linux process using the "dd"
//   Linux command.
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://www.levelblue.com/blogs/spiderlabs-blog/linux-based-inter-process-code-injection-without-ptrace-2/
//   - https://github.com/AonCyberLabs/Cexigua/blob/34d338620afae4c6335ba8d8d499e1d7d3d5d7b5/overwrite.sh
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/9493cf5c565d7a01b8f064e01a51e1b94057e4bb/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_dd_process_injection.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/dd" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "of=" and ProcessCommandLine contains "/proc/" and ProcessCommandLine contains "/mem")
